n the film. Below ~10 nm the film goes black (destructive interference of all visible λ)."}],"parameters":[{"id":"n-air","kind":"ior-real","unit":"dimensionless","value":1.0003},{"id":"n-film","kind":"ior-real","unit":"dimensionless","value":1.35,"range":[1.33,1.42],"notes":"Water plus surfactant. Lower than hydrocarbon oil."},{"id":"view-exp","kind":"weight","unit":"dimensionless","value":2.4},{"id":"view-lobe","kind":"lobe","unit":"enum","enum":"grazing"},{"id":"illuminant","kind":"illuminant","unit":"enum","enum":"D65"},{"id":"observer","kind":"observer","unit":"enum","enum":"CIE-1931-2deg"}],"nodes":[{"id":"film","operator":"thin-film-interference","bind":{"thickness":{"field":"film-thickness"},"nIncident":{"param":"n-air"},"nFilm":{"param":"n-film"},"nExit":{"param":"n-air"}}},{"id":"interface","operator":"fresnel-dielectric","bind":{"ior":{"param":"n-film"}}},{"id":"view","operator":"view-dependent-weight","bind":{"exponent":{"param":"view-exp"},"lobe":{"param":"view-lobe"}}},{"id":"display","operator":"spectral-integrate","bind":{"illuminant":{"param":"illuminant"},"observer":{"param":"observer"}}}],"graph":{"output":"display","edges":[{"from":"film","to":"view"},{"from":"interface","to":"view"},{"from":"view","to":"display"}]},"evaluation":{"illuminant":"D65","observer":"CIE-1931-2deg"},"notes":"Oil slick and soap film share thin-film-interference. They differ by n-exit (water vs air), n-film, thickness generator, and view lobe."}],"taxonomy":{"version":"0.1.0","id":"shader-grammar-material-taxonomy","principle":"Materials are classified by optical mechanism, not by trade name, pigment, or marketing effect. Words like iridescent, pearlescent, holographic, and opalescent are appearance aliases that map onto mechanisms. A specimen is valid only if every tag here resolves to a catalog operator.\n","families":[{"id":"interference","title":"Interference","description":"Color from optical-path phase in a film or stack. Thickness and IOR contrast are the degrees of freedom, not a hue slider.\n","members":[{"id":"thin-film","operators":["thin-film-interference"]},{"id":"multilayer","operators":["multilayer-interference"]}]},{"id":"diffraction","title":"Diffraction","description":"Color from periodic microstructure. Period, groove depth, and viewing angle set the orders — not a rainbow texture.\n","members":[{"id":"grating","operators":["diffraction-grating"]}]},{"id":"interface","title":"Interface","description":"Energy split at a boundary. Dielectrics use real IOR; conductors use complex IOR. Roughness is a separate geometric operator.\n","members":[{"id":"dielectric-interface","operators":["fresnel-dielectric"]},{"id":"conductor-interface","operators":["fresnel-conductor"]},{"id":"microfacet","operators":["microfacet-scatter"]}]},{"id":"geometric-transport","title":"Geometric transport","description":"Rays bent by a dielectric interface, then focused by a displacement field. Caustics are a consequence of refraction plus geometry.\n","members":[{"id":"refraction","operators":["refraction-snell"]},{"id":"caustics","operators":["caustic-transport"]}]},{"id":"volume","title":"Volume transport","description":"In-scattering, absorption, and emission in a participating medium. Phase function and density field do the work; cloud and nebula are not separate shader types.\n","members":[{"id":"scattering","operators":["volume-scattering"]},{"id":"absorption","operators":["volume-absorption"]},{"id":"emission","operators":["volume-emission"]}]},{"id":"evaluation","title":"Evaluation","description":"View weighting and spectral-to-display conversion. Shared by every recipe that produces color.\n","members":[{"id":"view-weight","operators":["view-dependent-weight"]},{"id":"display","operators":["spectral-integrate"]}]}],"aliases":{"iridescent":["thin-film","multilayer","grating"],"pearlescent":["multilayer"],"labradorescence":["multilayer"],"holographic":["grating","thin-film"],"opalescence":["scattering"],"metallic":["conductor-interface"